// Tuist tailnet ACLs.
//
// Source of truth for who-can-talk-to-what on the Tuist tailnet.
// The file lives in the repo so changes go through code review,
// not via someone's browser session.
//
// HOW IT REACHES THE TAILNET: today, by hand. Paste this file into
// the Tailscale admin console under "Access controls". Nothing in
// CI applies it, so a merged change to this file has NO effect
// until someone does that — a fact worth remembering when a fresh
// grant appears not to work.
//
// Manual is a DECISION, not a platform limitation, and not an
// oversight to go fix. Tailscale does expose the policy file over
// its API (`GET`/`POST /api/v2/tailnet/<tailnet>/acl`, accepting
// HuJSON and honouring `If-Match` for optimistic concurrency, plus
// an `/acl/validate` endpoint), and ships an official GitHub Action
// — `tailscale/gitops-acl-action` — that runs `action: test` on
// pull requests and `action: apply` on merge.
//
// We are not using it yet, because the `policy_file` scope is
// whole-file: there is no way to authorise a credential for "may
// edit the staging grants" and nothing else. A CI identity able to
// apply this file could therefore add a
// `tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes` grant on
// `tag:tuist-k8s-production` handing its holder
// `tuist-production-write` — exactly the elevation the JIT flow
// exists to gate behind a stated intent, a TTL and a second human.
// Automating the paste would put that capability behind "can land a
// commit on main" instead. The `tests` block below cannot backstop
// it either: Tailscale's test engine does not resolve `autogroup:*`,
// so the human-kubectl grants are precisely the ones an automated
// `apply` would validate the least.
//
// Revisit when both halves have an answer: federated identity
// (GitHub Actions OIDC, so no long-lived secret — see
// https://tailscale.com/kb/1581/workload-identity-federation) for
// the credential, and some way to keep production-tier grants off
// the automated path. Until then, a human pastes it.
//
// Note the OAuth client we already have, used by tuist-ops's
// TuistOps.JIT.TailscaleClient, is deliberately `users:read` only
// and cannot write this file.
//
// Tag taxonomy:
//
//   tag:tuist-mgmt           Tuist team's management devices
//                            (Talos node access, ops laptops).
//                            Inherited from the tailnet's initial
//                            setup.
//   tag:tuist-macmini-<env>  Mac minis in a specific env's macOS
//                            fleet (xcresult-processor, customer
//                            runners, future fleets), plus the
//                            xcresult-processor Tart VMs that share
//                            the fleet's credential. Joined via
//                            infra/macos-host-bootstrap; the env
//                            suffix is set by the operator-image
//                            flag --tailscale-tags, which the chart
//                            wires from macosFleet.tailscale.tags
//                            per env values file. Like the k8s tag
//                            below, each env joins with its own
//                            OAuth client scoped to this tag,
//                            never a pre-auth key, which Tailscale
//                            caps at 90 days and which took the
//                            xcresult queue offline when it lapsed
//                            (2026-08-12). Tags declared here are
//                            what an OAuth-minted key applies, so
//                            a tag removed from tagOwners breaks
//                            every subsequent join.
//   tag:tuist-k8s-<env>      Tailscale K8s operator + the cluster-
//                            side subnet-router Connector it
//                            provisions, scoped to one env's
//                            cluster. Each env runs its own
//                            operator instance with its own OAuth
//                            client, so a leaked staging credential
//                            can't enroll a device under a prod
//                            tag.
//   tag:tuist-ops            Human operators with SSH access to
//                            every macOS fleet, regardless of env.
//                            Env-agnostic deliberately — humans
//                            manage all envs, the tag-per-env
//                            split is for credential isolation,
//                            not human access boundaries.

{
	// Human access groups for the Kubernetes API server proxy.
	//
	// Identity for the cluster grants below is sourced from the
	// tailnet role (Owner/Admin/Member, set in the Tailscale admin
	// console Users page) via Tailscale's built-in autogroups:
	//   autogroup:owner  + autogroup:admin   → cluster admins
	//   autogroup:member                     → engineers
	// No email lists are kept in this file; promoting someone in
	// the console flips their access on the next session.
	//
	// For CANARY and PRODUCTION this document is the **view-tier**
	// policy only. Elevated access (writes) there flows through a
	// separate Pomerium gateway that gates per-request on a Tuist DB
	// flag — no Tailscale policy mutation. So there are no
	// "break-glass" groups declared here, and the ACL itself is
	// humans-only, edited through code review.
	//
	// STAGING is different: its write tier is standing access rather
	// than a per-request elevation, so there is nothing left for a
	// per-request gateway to decide and staging kubectl runs over
	// the tailnet instead. That makes the
	// `tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes` grants further down the
	// authoritative tier decision for staging — the only place in
	// this file that hands out an `edit`-tier group.
	"groups": {},

	"tagOwners": {
		"tag:tuist-mgmt":              ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-ops":               ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-k8s-staging":       ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-k8s-canary":        ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-k8s-production":    ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-macmini-staging":   ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-macmini-canary":    ["autogroup:admin"],
		"tag:tuist-macmini-production":["autogroup:admin"],
	},

	"grants": [
		// Catch-all wide-open rule inherited from the tailnet's
		// initial setup. Kept until a separate audit narrows it
		// without breaking management access (Talos node, ops
		// laptops). The explicit env-pair rules below are subsumed
		// by this today; they're documented so the macOS fleet
		// keeps working when this rule is removed.
		{"src": ["*"], "dst": ["*"], "ip": ["*"]},

		// Per-env operator → matching-env Mac mini fleet on the
		// scrape ports plus SSH:
		//   :22    tart-kubelet drift updates. The CAPI controller
		//          rolls host config to already-bootstrapped minis
		//          over the tailnet (reconcileTailscaleEgressService
		//          fronts :22 on the ProxyGroup) because a running
		//          runner mini filters inbound :22 on its public
		//          interface, wedging the public-IP roll path.
		//   :8080  controller-runtime metrics on tart-kubelet
		//          (reconcile counts, errors, and the
		//          tart_kubelet_vm_boot_duration_seconds histogram).
		//   :9091  PromEx on the xcresult-processor Tart VM,
		//          fronted by tart-kubelet's host-side Forwarder.
		//   :9100  node_exporter on the Mac mini host.
		//
		// Cross-env scraping (staging operator → prod macmini) is
		// blocked at the tailnet layer once the wide-open rule
		// above is removed. Today it's blocked in practice anyway
		// because the CAPI controller in env X only materialises
		// per-mini egress Services for env X's Mac minis (each
		// cluster runs its own controller against its own set of
		// ScalewayAppleSiliconMachines), so alloy-metrics never
		// gets a cross-env target to dial. The explicit per-pair
		// grants here make the boundary load-bearing rather than
		// coincidental.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9091", "tcp:9100"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9091", "tcp:9100"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:22", "tcp:8080", "tcp:9091", "tcp:9100"],
		},

		// Same-env Mac mini fleet → CNPG pooler proxy on tcp:5432.
		// The Tailscale operator-managed proxy (rendered with the
		// `tailscale.com/expose` + `tailscale.com/tags` annotations
		// in postgresql-cnpg-pooler.yaml) registers under
		// `tag:tuist-k8s-<env>` — the operator's OAuth client is
		// only authorised to mint that tag today, so the proxy
		// shares it with the cluster's subnet router rather than
		// carrying a dedicated `tag:tuist-pg-pooler-<env>` tag.
		// That makes the dst here wider than the proxy alone, but
		// the catch-all above already subsumes that today; this is
		// the load-bearing rule for when the catch-all is removed.
		// Follow-up to narrow further: provision a dedicated proxy
		// tag in the OAuth client, list it under tagOwners below,
		// and swap the `tailscale.com/tags` annotation on the
		// Pooler CR to use it.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			// 5432: CNPG pooler proxy. 4317 and 3100: the Alloy receiver
			// proxy, for spans and logs. Both the Mac mini HOST and the
			// Tart VMs on it push logs to 3100. The VMs have no route to
			// the in-cluster receiver address other workloads use; the
			// host's own launchd logs (tart-kubelet's reconciler, node
			// agent and volume manager, shipped by
			// infra/macos-log-shipper) cannot be tailed from the cluster
			// at all, because a Pod on a macOS Node IS a VM.
			// :443   the OCI registry holding the VM images. The
			//        builders clone their base and push finished
			//        images over this; without it the image
			//        workflows lose their registry the moment the
			//        catch-all grant above is removed.
			"ip":  ["tcp:5432", "tcp:4317", "tcp:3100", "tcp:443"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			// 5432: CNPG pooler proxy. 4317 and 3100: the Alloy receiver
			// proxy, for spans and logs. Both the Mac mini HOST and the
			// Tart VMs on it push logs to 3100. The VMs have no route to
			// the in-cluster receiver address other workloads use; the
			// host's own launchd logs (tart-kubelet's reconciler, node
			// agent and volume manager, shipped by
			// infra/macos-log-shipper) cannot be tailed from the cluster
			// at all, because a Pod on a macOS Node IS a VM.
			// :443   the OCI registry holding the VM images. The
			//        builders clone their base and push finished
			//        images over this; without it the image
			//        workflows lose their registry the moment the
			//        catch-all grant above is removed.
			"ip":  ["tcp:5432", "tcp:4317", "tcp:3100", "tcp:443"],
		},
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			// 5432: CNPG pooler proxy. 4317 and 3100: the Alloy receiver
			// proxy, for spans and logs. Both the Mac mini HOST and the
			// Tart VMs on it push logs to 3100. The VMs have no route to
			// the in-cluster receiver address other workloads use; the
			// host's own launchd logs (tart-kubelet's reconciler, node
			// agent and volume manager, shipped by
			// infra/macos-log-shipper) cannot be tailed from the cluster
			// at all, because a Pod on a macOS Node IS a VM.
			// :443   the OCI registry holding the VM images. The
			//        builders clone their base and push finished
			//        images over this; without it the image
			//        workflows lose their registry the moment the
			//        catch-all grant above is removed.
			"ip":  ["tcp:5432", "tcp:4317", "tcp:3100", "tcp:443"],
		},

		// Staging Mac mini fleet → the staging cluster's Service CIDR,
		// through the cluster-side subnet-router Connector
		// (infra/helm/tailscale-operator/values-staging.yaml advertises
		// 10.128.0.0/12; infra/k8s/clusters/cluster-staging.yaml is the
		// source of truth for the CIDR). This is the macOS runner-cache
		// path: Tart runner VMs on the minis dial the account's private
		// Kura node at its ClusterIP — 4000 (the co-hosted HTTP + gRPC
		// cache port), mirroring the Linux runner namespace's egress
		// carve-out in infra/helm/tuist/templates/runners-namespace.yaml
		// — plus cluster DNS on 53 (kube-dns ClusterIP lives in the same
		// range) so the VM's /etc/resolver entry can resolve
		// `*.svc.cluster.local` names. Subnet-routed destinations are
		// matched by CIDR, not by the router's tag, hence the literal
		// prefix in dst. Per-account isolation is enforced at the Kura
		// app layer (Kura's authorization verifies the build's JWT),
		// same as for Linux runners.
		//
		// Staging only for now: all envs share this Service CIDR, so the
		// CIDR-dst grant can't distinguish envs — it points at whichever
		// env's Connector advertises the route. Keep this aligned with
		// the single-advertiser rule documented in the tailscale-operator
		// chart values before adding canary/production entries.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"dst": ["10.128.0.0/12"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:4000", "tcp:53", "udp:53"],
		},

		// Humans SSH into any Mac mini. Not load-bearing — the
		// CAPI provider's per-fleet SSH key is the normal path the
		// controller takes. tag:tuist-ops is env-agnostic because
		// the same humans manage all envs.
		{
			"src": ["tag:tuist-ops"],
			"dst": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production",
			],
			"ip": ["tcp:22"],
		},

		// Human kubectl to CANARY and PRODUCTION is NOT a tailnet
		// grant — Pomerium per env handles it via Google Workspace
		// OIDC (`kube-<env>.tuist.dev`) + per-request impersonation
		// resolved by tuist-ops's PolicyController, which reads the
		// live JIT elevation row. That decision cannot be expressed
		// in a static ACL, which is exactly why those two envs stay
		// behind the gateway. No `tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes`
		// grants for `tag:tuist-k8s-canary` or
		// `tag:tuist-k8s-production` belong in this file.

		// Human kubectl to STAGING, on the other hand, IS a tailnet
		// grant. Staging's write tier is standing access rather than
		// a per-request elevation
		// (`TuistOps.JIT.Policy.always_write_env?/1`), so there is no
		// per-request lookup left to do and no reason to make anyone
		// re-authenticate to a browser gateway for it. The staging
		// operator runs Tailscale's in-process API server proxy in
		// auth mode (`apiServerProxyConfig.mode: "true"` in
		// infra/helm/tailscale-operator/values-staging.yaml) at
		// `tuist-k8s-staging.taild6d7bb.ts.net`; it WhoIs's the
		// caller and turns the `impersonate.groups` below into the
		// `Impersonate-Group` headers the apiserver binds.
		//
		// The group names are the same ones Pomerium's chart binds to
		// ClusterRoles in
		// infra/helm/pomerium/templates/access-tiers.yaml — that file
		// is now load-bearing for BOTH doors into staging, so it must
		// not be deleted along with a future Pomerium teardown.
		//
		// KNOWN GAP vs the gateway path: `autogroup:member` is
		// role-agnostic — Tailscale documents it as every direct
		// member of the tailnet, which includes admin-flavour roles
		// (Auditor, Billing admin, IT admin, Network admin).
		// tuist-ops's PolicyController denies those roles outright.
		// So a non-engineering tailnet role that would get nothing
		// through Pomerium gets `tuist-eng` + `tuist-staging-write`
		// here. Accepted for staging while every member of this
		// tailnet is an engineer. The fix, if that stops being true,
		// is to declare an explicit `group:tuist-eng` in the `groups`
		// block above and use it as `src` instead of the autogroup —
		// at the cost of keeping an email list in this file.
		//
		// Owners and Admins match BOTH grants (they are members too),
		// so they receive `tuist-admins` + `tuist-eng` +
		// `tuist-staging-write`. Harmless: `tuist-admins` and
		// `tuist-eng` both bind to `view`, and the union is the same
		// tier either way.
		//
		// `ip: tcp:443` is what actually reaches the proxy. It is
		// subsumed by the catch-all grant at the top of this list
		// today; it is spelled out so the path survives that rule's
		// removal, same convention as the pairs above. Note the dst
		// tag is shared with the cluster's subnet router and egress
		// proxies, so this opens 443 slightly wider than the operator
		// alone — the same caveat already recorded for the CNPG
		// pooler grant.
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:443"],
			"app": {
				"tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes": [
					{"impersonate": {"groups": ["tuist-eng", "tuist-staging-write"]}},
				],
			},
		},
		{
			"src": ["autogroup:owner", "autogroup:admin"],
			"dst": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"ip":  ["tcp:443"],
			"app": {
				"tailscale.com/cap/kubernetes": [
					{"impersonate": {"groups": ["tuist-admins", "tuist-staging-write"]}},
				],
			},
		},

		// Elevated (`edit`) access for CANARY and PRODUCTION does not
		// live here. It flows through the Pomerium gateway (see
		// `infra/helm/pomerium/`), which authenticates callers via
		// Google Workspace OIDC, looks up the active elevation in
		// the tuist-ops DB per request, and injects the
		// Impersonate-Group header that maps to the env's elevated
		// ClusterRole. For those two envs the tailnet ACL stays
		// read-only and static — no runtime mutation, no break-glass
		// groups, and no role-tier branching at this layer (Pomerium
		// owns that decision).
	],

	// Subnet-route auto-approval, so the staging Connector's
	// advertised Service CIDR goes live on `helm upgrade` without a
	// human clicking "approve" in the admin console (the Connector
	// Pod re-advertises on every recreate; manual approval would
	// break the route on each rollout). Scoped to the staging
	// cluster tag — the 100.64.0.0/10 advertisement predates this
	// file's autoApprovers section and stays manually approved.
	"autoApprovers": {
		"routes": {
			"10.128.0.0/12": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
		},
	},

	// Tailscale SSH (distinct from "grants"). The same-env
	// k8s→macmini accept grants let the cluster-side subnet-router
	// `tailscale ssh admin@<vm-tailnet-ip>` into matching-env Mac
	// mini hosts + the Tart VMs that run on them — diagnostic
	// shell access when `kubectl logs` / `kubectl exec` against
	// the kubelet endpoint fails (tart-kubelet's Node hostname
	// doesn't resolve from the K8s apiserver's DNS, so the
	// standard debug path is broken on macOS fleets and this is
	// the substitute).
	//
	// Locked down to the `admin` user only — the bake-time user
	// that owns /opt/tuist and the launchd plist. Explicitly
	// excludes `root` and the broader `autogroup:nonroot` to keep
	// a compromise of the k8s tag (large blast radius — cluster
	// subnet router, every operator pod the Tailscale operator
	// exposes) from being a root-shell pivot. Tagged-device SSH
	// can't use `check` mode (no re-auth flow for a non-human
	// source), so `accept` is the only option and the narrowing
	// happens via `users`. See sshTests below.
	"ssh": [
		{
			"action": "check",
			"src":    ["autogroup:member"],
			"dst":    ["autogroup:self"],
			"users":  ["autogroup:nonroot", "root"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging"],
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"users":  ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary"],
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"users":  ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"action": "accept",
			"src":    ["tag:tuist-k8s-production"],
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"users":  ["admin"],
		},
	],

	// SSH ACL assertions, enforced on every save in the admin
	// console. The accept lines lock in the `admin`-only narrowing
	// added with #11134's diagnostic grants; the reject lines
	// catch the failure modes that would re-expand blast radius
	// — root SSH (host take-over) or cross-env SSH (staging
	// subnet router into production VMs).
	"sshTests": [
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"accept": ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-staging"],
			"deny":   ["root"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"deny":   ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"accept": ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-canary"],
			"deny":   ["root"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"accept": ["admin"],
		},
		{
			"src":    "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"dst":    ["tag:tuist-macmini-production"],
			"deny":   ["root"],
		},
	],

	// Tests run on every save in the console. With the wide-open
	// rule above active, nothing is denied for any source, so these
	// only assert that the explicit env-pair grants parse and
	// accept the intended traffic. Add cross-env deny assertions
	// when the catch-all is removed.
	"tests": [
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-staging",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:8080",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:9091",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:9100",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-canary",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:8080",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:9091",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:9100",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-k8s-production",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:8080",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:9091",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:9100",
			],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-ops",
			"accept": [
				"tag:tuist-macmini-staging:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-canary:22",
				"tag:tuist-macmini-production:22",
			],
		},
		// macOS runner-cache path: staging minis reach the staging
		// cluster's Service CIDR on the Kura + DNS ports.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-staging",
			"accept": [
				"10.128.0.10:4000",
				"10.128.0.10:53",
			],
		},
		// Host-log push path: every mini POSTs its tart-kubelet log to
		// the same-env Alloy receiver's loki.source.api on 3100. The
		// grant predates the host agent (the Tart guests used it for
		// spans and logs), so this only pins it — a narrowing that
		// dropped 3100 would silently take the macOS fleet's only
		// queryable output offline, with a healthy-looking Node.
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-staging",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-staging:3100"],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-canary",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-canary:3100"],
		},
		{
			"src": "tag:tuist-macmini-production",
			"accept": ["tag:tuist-k8s-production:3100"],
		},
		// Note: no test entries with autogroup-based src. Tailscale's
		// test engine doesn't resolve `autogroup:*` as a concrete
